---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import Wordmark from "../../components/Wordmark.astro";
---

<Base
  title="Prompt placeholders — sudonotes"
  description="Write {{placeholders}} in a prompt and fill them in from the sidebar when you use it. The note stays a reusable template; only the copy on your clipboard is filled in."
>
  <section class="page" style="padding-block: clamp(44px, 7vw, 76px)">
    <div class="prose">
      <p class="eyebrow">Docs</p>
      <h1>Prompt placeholders</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        Most prompts are almost the same every time. One name changes, or one file path, or one
        tone. Placeholders let you write the prompt once and fill in the parts that move.
      </p>

      <h2>Writing one</h2>
      <p>
        In any prompt, put a name in double curly braces. That is the whole syntax:
      </p>

      <pre><code>Review the diff on {'{{branch}}'} and write release notes
for {'{{audience}}'}. Keep it under {'{{word count}}'} words.</code></pre>

      <p>
        Names can contain spaces, so <code>{'{{word count}}'}</code> is fine. Use the same name
        twice and it stays one field — fill it once and both copies take the value.
      </p>

      <h2>Filling it in</h2>
      <p>
        Open the prompt and look at the <strong>Variables</strong> panel on the right. Every
        placeholder in the note gets a field, listed in the order it first appears. Type your
        values, then press <strong>Copy filled</strong> — the filled prompt goes to your clipboard,
        ready to paste into whichever model the prompt is pointed at.
      </p>

      <div class="note">
        The note on disk never changes. Filling in values does not rewrite the prompt, so the
        template survives being used — that is the point of it. Values are remembered while the app
        is open and forgotten when you close it.
      </div>

      <h2>Leaving one blank</h2>
      <p>
        A placeholder you have not filled is copied through as-is, still in its braces, and the
        button tells you how many are still blank. A half-filled prompt therefore shows you exactly
        what is missing instead of pasting an empty gap into your chat window.
      </p>

      <h2>What it looks like in the file</h2>
      <p>
        Nothing special. Placeholders are ordinary text in the Markdown body, so a prompt with them
        is still a plain file you can read, edit in any editor, or grep:
      </p>

      <pre><code>---
id: 01J9F...
title: Release notes
type: prompt
model: anthropic/claude-opus-5
---

Review the diff on {'{{branch}}'} and write release notes
for {'{{audience}}'}.</code></pre>

      <p>
        There is no separate schema, no registry of variables, and nothing to migrate. If you delete
        a placeholder from the text, its field disappears from the panel.
      </p>

      <h2>Where they work</h2>
      <p>
        Placeholders are a prompt feature. Idea notes do not get a Variables panel — an idea is
        something you are still thinking through, not a template you run. If you want a template out
        of an idea, copy the bubble into a prompt and add the braces there.
      </p>

      <p>
        <Wordmark /> highlights <code>{'{{like this}}'}</code> in the editor so a template is
        recognisable at a glance rather than only when you go looking for the panel.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
